Context: Grothendieck's 1986 axiomatic 'analysis situs'. M is a set of elementary pieces, roughly closed cells. The family \U0001D4D5 consists of 'figures' F, which are subsets of M closed under ≤, comparable to subcomplexes. The relations in play are: ≤ (being a face/subpiece), ≪ (being a refinement/subdivision), |∘| (an interior-incidence relation between pieces), ∥ (disjointness), and the negated relation written ≭ (roughly 'interiors overlap / not separated'). Raff(F) = {G ∈ \U0001D4D5 | G ≪ F} is the set of refinements of F. The previous page proved Prop. 4: for a cover F = ∪F_i, Raff is a sheaf, Raff(F) = eq(∏ Raff(F_i) ⇉ ∏ Raff(F_i ∩ F_j)), and hence Raff(F) = lim_{X ∈ F̃} Raff(X).

This page states Lemma 2, an addendum that 'should come after p. 3'. Let G ≪ F = ∪F_i. Then G ≤ F iff G|F_i ≤ F_i for all i. If G, G' ≪ F, then G ≭ G' iff G|F_i ≭ G'|F_i for all i, and likewise for ∥, ≤, ≪. In modern terms, the relations between two subdivisions of a complex are local for a cover by subcomplexes. So Raff is not merely a sheaf of sets but a sheaf of 'relational structures'.

Grothendieck then comments that all this is recapitulative. It does not explicitly involve |∘|, except through ≭. He then sets a programme:
1) revisit the axioms for 'lieux' (points, i.e. ≪-minimal elements), open sets and 'multiouverts';
2) revisit the set-theoretic and quasi-set-theoretic models, where the lieux dominate;
3) above all, redo the theory of 'segments';
4) treat what he calls the most common case. There ≪ is definable from ≤ and |∘|. To formulate this he needs a formal interior X° = X \ ∂X for X ∈ M and an 'inclusion' X° ⊂ Y° of interiors, which requires 3) first.

He decides to treat 1) and 2) now; the next page begins the axioms for lieux, with umb(X), umb(X)° and the axiom MagL1.
